Oh no, the Python script made a mess! For multi-line type definitions, it only matched the first line and appended the workaround after the first line, leaving the rest of the type definition dangling. For example:

```
export type NavGraphContract = { buildings: NavGraphContractNavBuilding[] };
export const NavGraphContract = undefined as unknown as NavGraphContract; { buildings: NavGraphContractNavBuilding[] };
export const NavGraphContract = undefined as unknown as NavGraphContract;
```

And for single-line types with `;`:
```
export type BootstrapPhase = 'idle' | 'loading' | 'ready' | 'error';


export const BootstrapPhase = undefined as unknown as BootstrapPhase; 'idle' | 'loading' | 'ready' | 'error';
export const BootstrapPhase = undefined as unknown as BootstrapPhase;
```

The issue is that the regex only captures the first line with `export type Name =`, but multi-line definitions continue on subsequent lines, so the workaround gets inserted after just the first line instead of after the complete type definition. For single-line types with semicolons, the workaround ends up on the same line as the type content. I need to rewrite the script to properly handle both cases.

Actually, the cleanest approach is to read each file, identify every `export type Name =` declaration whether single or multi-line, and insert the workaround right after the entire declaration closes with a semicolon. I'll also need to check `navGraph.ts` to ensure it doesn't already have the workaround applied where it shouldn't be.

